@meta
id: 01-01
title: The editor that types back
chapter: 01
type: lesson
skills: insert-i, esc, cmdline, mode-awareness
par: 16
minutes: 3
vscode: clicking in the file and typing
open: standup.md

@brief
Open standup.md and you are in NORMAL mode. Nothing you type will appear.
That is not a fault. In normal mode every letter on the keyboard is a
command, which is why vim can do so much with so few keys. You type text
only after you have asked to.

The title line is missing its first word. It should read:

    Daily notes for Tuesday standup

Add the missing word, get back out of insert mode, and save.

The cursor already starts exactly where the word goes, so you do not need
to move at all yet.

@keys
| `i` | insert — start typing to the left of the cursor |
| `Esc` | leave insert mode, back to normal mode |
| `:w` | write (save) |
| `:q` | quit |
| `:wq` | both, the one you will type a thousand times |

@hints
Press `i` first. The bottom of the screen will say `-- INSERT --`. Now
your typing goes into the file.
--
When the word is typed, press `Esc`. The `-- INSERT --` marker disappears
and you are back in normal mode.
--
From normal mode, type `:wq` and press Enter. The colon moves you to the
command line at the bottom; `w` writes, `q` quits.

@solution
    i Daily <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Sixteen keystrokes, twelve of them the word itself.

The thing to take from this is the shape, not the keys. You *entered* a
mode, did one small job, and *left* it. Beginners coming from VS Code tend
to press `i` once and stay in insert mode all day, using arrow keys to get
around. That works, and it throws away every advantage vim has.

Normal mode is home. Insert mode is somewhere you visit to type a few
words, then leave. If you are ever lost or confused, `Esc` twice puts you
back on solid ground, and `u` undoes whatever you just did.

@start standup.md
notes for Tuesday standup

Yesterday: shipped the checkout fix
Today: reviewing the payments PR
Blocked: nothing

@expected standup.md
Daily notes for Tuesday standup

Yesterday: shipped the checkout fix
Today: reviewing the payments PR
Blocked: nothing
